[
  {
    "title": "Germ Theory As Conspiracy",
    "body": "Leach turns the usual framing inside out, arguing that if a conspiracy theory is something unfalsifiable and ungrounded in evidence, then germ theory fits that bill perfectly. He points to more than two hundred contagion studies, including the Spanish flu experiments in which over a hundred prisoners sat close to hospitalized patients, swapped spit and were coughed on, and, he says, walked away with zero infections. He cites Daniel Roytas and the book Can You Catch a Cold, and maintains that bacteria, which outnumber our cells by something like ten or a hundred to one, consume only diseased and decaying tissue as nature's cleanup crew."
  },
  {
    "title": "Six Feet Of Biofield",
    "body": "Both men replace the invisible particle with resonance, describing the measurable electromagnetic field generated by the body's electrical activity, a torus said to extend roughly six feet out. Leach recounts Eileen McKusick striking a tuning fork and hearing the tone shift before it ever reached the body, and describes Russian experiments in which cell cultures sealed in quartz vials broke down in sympathy while cultures in ordinary glass, which blocks ultraviolet light, did not. Chance offers copper L rods as a home test, and recalls locating from Seattle to Missouri the exact year a fifty seven year old client was braced for scoliosis."
  },
  {
    "title": "Teflon Fumes And Holiday Sugar",
    "body": "Instead of something going around, Leach proposes shared exposure. He revisits the decades when he says seventy percent or more of American households cooked on Teflon, whose coating releases invisible odorless fumes past five hundred degrees, producing what was named polymer fume fever with symptoms of fever, chills and headache that a family would blame on little Jimmy at school. He does the same with a Chick-fil-A dinner and its more than fifty artificial ingredients, and with flu season, tracing Halloween candy through Thanksgiving pie to Christmas cookies alongside cold dry air and the mucus the body makes in response."
  },
  {
    "title": "As Above So Below",
    "body": "Leach says he ran an experiment to see whether the terrain paradigm holds up against the hermetic principles and concluded that it does, tugging first on the law of cause and effect against what he calls the sloppy causal chains proposed by germ theory. Under correspondence, he points to German New Medicine and Dr. Hamer, who he says mapped tens of thousands of scans showing concentric rings in brain regions corresponding to particular organs and particular shock events. Chance extends the fractal to iridology, reflexology and the ear that looks like an upside down fetus, and both bring in osteopathy and biogeometry."
  },
  {
    "title": "Root Cause Over Symptom Suppression",
    "body": "Leach maps a client's terrain across three drivers he names as toxicities, deficiencies, and trauma or stress, calling himself a medical detective who checks sunlight, fresh air, spring water, whole foods and daily movement before anything else. He describes stuck emotion as acting like radiation in the body, crediting his mentor Dr. Lando, and invokes the Buddhist first arrow followed by the arrows we keep shooting at ourselves, plus Chinese medicine placing anger in the liver and grief in the lungs. Chance warns against green allopathy, and Leach closes on Carl Jung, the unmet task of the heart, and know thyself."
  }
]

